.TH TIMES 2
.SH NAME
times \- get process times
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/times.h>
.PP
.B int times(struct tms *buffer);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Times
returns time-accounting information
for the current process
and for the terminated child processes
of the current process.
All times are in 1/HZ seconds.
.B Note:
.B HZ is 250 here,
which is what this kernel programs the clock to and what CLOCKS_PER_SEC in
<time.h> is defined as.
.PP
After the call, the buffer will appear as follows:
.PP
.nf
struct tms {
	time_t	tms_utime;	/* user time */
	time_t	tms_stime;	/* system time */
	time_t	tms_cutime;	/* user time, children */
	time_t	tms_cstime;	/* system time, children */
};
.PP
.fi
.B Note:
v7's page called the struct
.I tbuffer
and its members
.IR proc_user_time
and so on.  The names here are the ones <sys/times.h> declares, which are the
names v7's own kernel and successors use.
The children times are the sum
of the children's process times and
their children's times.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
time(1), time(2), ctime(3)
.PP
.B Note:
<sys/times.h> declares
.I times
now; v7 declared it in no header and every caller wrote its own extern.  The
declaration is guarded, because the kernel has a handler of the same name with a
different shape and the two must never both be in scope.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_times
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
